+# smallpt browser playground
+
+Kevin Beason’s [smallpt](https://www.kevinbeason.com/smallpt/) C++ path tracer,
+compiled to WebAssembly with Emscripten. Open `index.html` through a static HTTP(S)
+host; no application backend or cross-origin isolation headers are required.
+The generated `.wasm` and ES module are included in `assets/`.
+
+The page renders automatically. Randomize & render changes the two sphere colors
+and the two side walls, then starts a fresh render. Each swatch also opens a color
+picker. Quality and resolution controls rerender the scene; reset restores defaults.
+Save image exports the completed canvas as PNG. The expand icon uses fullscreen
+when supported.
+
+## Build
+
+Requires Emscripten (`em++`) on PATH:
+
+```sh
+npm run build
+npm test
+```
+
+The build compiles `src/renderer.cpp` into `assets/smallpt.js` and
+`assets/smallpt.wasm`. A module Web Worker runs the C++ integrator and transfers
+RGBA image buffers to the canvas after each four-sample pass. Re-rendering
+terminates the previous worker. This keeps the UI responsive and needs no
+SharedArrayBuffer or pthreads.
+
+The ray intersection, recursive radiance, mirror/glass materials, camera, tent
+filter, and scene geometry come from smallpt. The original CLI loop is replaced
+by an API for progressive accumulation and editable reflectance colors. Separate
+subpixel accumulators preserve smallpt’s averaging and clamping behavior.
+`vendor/smallpt/smallpt.cpp` is the unmodified upstream reference; its MIT license
+is in `vendor/smallpt/LICENSE.txt`.
+
+`assets/preview.png` was rendered by this same Wasm module at 640×480 / 128 spp.
+It remains visible until the first live frame arrives. To regenerate its raw RGBA
+buffer, run `node scripts/test-renderer.mjs --preview`; the buffer is saved to
+`.build/preview.rgba`. It can be encoded as a 640×480 RGBA PNG with any image tool.
+Fonts load from Google Fonts, with local sans-serif fallbacks.
+
+Validation executes the compiled Wasm and actual Worker module in Node without
+opening a browser or starting a server.
